# Binary files git must never touch.
#
# **`core.autocrlf` is true on this machine and nothing told git these were binary.** Git's own
# heuristic missed them, so every checkout rewrote PDFs and Office documents with CRLF applied: on
# 18 September, 21 tracked files under `ticvai/sources/` differed from their own blob, twelve of
# them PDFs, 141,748 bytes added in total.
#
# **The blobs were always correct** — `ACCREDITATION.pdf` in git is byte-identical to the client's
# original. The damage happened on the way out, which is worse than it sounds: a document that
# round-trips through a checkout no longer hashes to what the client sent, so "is this the file
# they gave us" cannot be answered by comparing hashes. That is how four broken PDFs in
# `sources/requirements/` sat unnoticed in the repository.
#
# Adding this file does not repair a working tree. The affected paths must be rewritten from their
# blobs afterwards; nothing needs recommitting, because the blobs were never wrong.

*.pdf   binary
*.docx  binary
*.doc   binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.xls   binary
*.pptx  binary
*.ppt   binary
*.zip   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.gz    binary
